Deliver touch gestures to targets inside a shadow root.

On a touch screen the editor's context menu cannot be scrolled with a finger.
The menu a long press opens holds 25 items; with the on-screen keyboard up it
has 406px for 542px of rows, and the only way to the rest is the thin scrollbar
on its edge.

The chain: Gesture listens for touch events on the document, takes each
touch's target as the gesture's initial target, and dispatches tap, pan and
context menu events to every registered target that contains it. The editor
opens its context menu in a shadow root (EditorOption.useShadowDOM). A touch
that starts in there reaches a document listener retargeted to the root's
host, and nothing inside the root contains its own host, so the menu and its
items, registered targets all, are never dispatched to and the menu's pan
handler never runs. Items still ran, through the mouse events the browser
synthesises for a touch nobody handled.

Measured on an API 36 emulator (WebView 134) with a real swipe of 244px inside
the menu: touch.target was the shadow host while the composed path began at
the item's label, no gesture event reached the root, the touch events were
left unhandled, and the menu stayed at 0. The explorer's context menu is the
same Menu class in the light DOM; in landscape, where both overflow, one drag
scrolled it 199px and left the editor's at 0.

The composed path of the touchstart still begins at the element that was
touched, so the initial target is read from there. For a touch outside any
shadow root that is the element Touch.target already named. With the change
the same swipe scrolled the editor's menu to its end (138px), a tap on its
last item ran it, a submenu opened and ran its own item, and a tap outside
closed the menu. Taps inside the menu now arrive as tap gestures, as they
already do in every light-DOM menu, in place of the synthesised mouse events.

Other menus opened in a shadow root reach Gesture the same way; only the
editor's context menu was measured. Node.contains does not cross a shadow
boundary, so a registered target outside the root no longer receives a
gesture that began inside it. None did in the measurement above, where the
touch went unhandled.

